explanation:
The issue is caused by attempting to call getClass() on null elements in the array. The for loop should be modified to check if each element is null before calling getClass(). If an element is null, it should be set to null instead of trying to access its class.

For example, the line `classes[i] = array[i].getClass();` should be changed to `if (array[i] != null) { classes[i] = array[i].getClass(); } else { classes[i] = null; }

This will prevent the NullPointerException that is currently occurring when trying to call getClass() on a null element.

Additionally, the loop should check if it's going out of bounds before accessing elements in the array. The line `for (int i = 0; i < array.length; i++)` does not need to be changed, but a check should be added inside the loop to ensure that `i` is within the bounds of the array.

Here is the corrected code:

Object[] array = new Object[]{String.class, null, Double.class};
Class<?>[] classes = new Class[array.length];
for (int i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
    if (array[i] != null) {
        classes[i] = array[i].getClass();
    } else {
        classes[i] = null;
    }
}

String input = "Test";
Double doubleValue = 99d;
new Object[]{input,doubleValue};
